@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `mw-il32-nouns`
IMPORT `mw-s1-definitions`
IMPORT `mw-s2-the-right`
IMPORT `mw-s3-pay-that-counts`
IMPORT `mw-il32-published-figures`
IMPORT `mw-il32-answers`

-- Tests for the Minimum Wage Law 5747-1987 (row IL-32). Every expected value was worked out by hand
-- from the Hebrew text before the assertion was run; the working is written beside each group. None was
-- copied from an evaluation. A failing assertion is a finding about the text or the encoding, never a
-- value to edit.
--
-- Two kinds of expected value appear:
--   [text]:  arithmetic or a rule read directly off the Law (source line given);
--   [pub]:   a figure the Institute publishes (provenance in mw-il32-published-figures.l4), which is
--            not law. Every [pub] assertion is about the published figure, not the Law's rule.

§ `Fixtures`

GIVETH AN `An employee`
`a monthly-paid adult` MEANS
    `An employee` WITH
        `age in completed years` IS 30
        `how pay is fixed` IS `paid by the month`
        `the working week` IS `a six-day working week`
        `within a class for which section 17 regulations may fix a lower rate` IS FALSE

GIVETH AN `An employee`
`a daily-paid adult on a six-day week` MEANS
    `An employee` WITH
        `age in completed years` IS 30
        `how pay is fixed` IS `paid by the day`
        `the working week` IS `a six-day working week`
        `within a class for which section 17 regulations may fix a lower rate` IS FALSE

GIVETH AN `An employee`
`a daily-paid adult on a five-day week` MEANS
    `An employee` WITH
        `age in completed years` IS 30
        `how pay is fixed` IS `paid by the day`
        `the working week` IS `a five-day working week`
        `within a class for which section 17 regulations may fix a lower rate` IS FALSE

GIVETH AN `An employee`
`an hourly-paid adult` MEANS
    `An employee` WITH
        `age in completed years` IS 30
        `how pay is fixed` IS `paid by the hour`
        `the working week` IS `a five-day working week`
        `within a class for which section 17 regulations may fix a lower rate` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the age` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH AN `An employee`
`a monthly-paid employee aged` `the age` MEANS
    `An employee` WITH
        `age in completed years` IS `the age`
        `how pay is fixed` IS `paid by the month`
        `the working week` IS `a six-day working week`
        `within a class for which section 17 regulations may fix a lower rate` IS FALSE

GIVETH AN `An employee`
`a monthly-paid adult in a section 17 class` MEANS
    `An employee` WITH
        `age in completed years` IS 30
        `how pay is fixed` IS `paid by the month`
        `the working week` IS `a six-day working week`
        `within a class for which section 17 regulations may fix a lower rate` IS TRUE

GIVEN `the hours` IS A NUMBER
      `the full position` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH AN `Hours of work in a month`
`the hours` `worked against a full position of` `the full position` MEANS
    `Hours of work in a month` WITH
        `hours the employee works in the month` IS `the hours`
        `hours of a full position customary at the workplace in the month` IS `the full position`

§ `Section 1 [text]`

-- 0.475 x 20,000 = 9,500 (20,000 / 2 = 10,000; 20,000 x 0.025 = 500; 10,000 - 500 = 9,500).
#ASSERT `the monthly minimum wage on 1 April, before increases under section 4, from` 20_000 EQUALS 9_500

-- Cross-check against the published figure [pub]: 47.5 percent of the published average wage of
-- 13,566 is 6,443.85 (13,566 / 2 = 6,783; 13,566 x 0.025 = 339.15; 6,783 - 339.15 = 6,443.85), which is
-- the figure published from 1 April 2026.
#ASSERT `the monthly minimum wage on 1 April, before increases under section 4, from` `the average wage from 1 January 2026, as published` EQUALS `the monthly minimum wage for an employee aged 18 or over from 1 April 2026, as published`

-- Daily, six-day week: a 25th. 6,500 / 25 = 260.
#ASSERT `the daily minimum wage from` 6_500 `for` `a six-day working week` EQUALS 260
-- Daily, five-day week: a 21 2/3rd, i.e. 65/3 of a month's... a month is 21 2/3 days: 6,500 / (65/3) = 6,500 x 3 / 65 = 300.
#ASSERT `the daily minimum wage from` 6_500 `for` `a five-day working week` EQUALS 300
-- The published monthly figure, six-day week: 6,443.85 / 25 = 257.754 (6,443.85 / 5 = 1,288.77; 1,288.77 / 5 = 257.754).
#ASSERT `the daily minimum wage from` 6_443.85 `for` `a six-day working week` EQUALS 257.754
-- Hourly: a 186th. 9,300 / 186 = 50.
#ASSERT `the hourly minimum wage from` 9_300 EQUALS 50
#ASSERT `the hours in a month that the hourly minimum wage assumes` EQUALS 186

-- s 5: an update never reduces the figure. Before 6,247.67; the update computes 6,000 -> 6,247.67; computes 6,500 -> 6,500; computes the same -> the same.
#ASSERT `the monthly minimum wage after an update, given` 6_247.67 `and` 6_000 EQUALS 6_247.67
#ASSERT `the monthly minimum wage after an update, given` 6_247.67 `and` 6_500 EQUALS 6_500
#ASSERT `the monthly minimum wage after an update, given` 6_247.67 `and` 6_247.67 EQUALS 6_247.67

-- s 21(a): 525 on commencement day, 551 on 1 October 1987.
#ASSERT `the monthly minimum wage that section 21(a)(1) fixes for 1 April 1987` EQUALS 525
#ASSERT `the monthly minimum wage that section 21(a)(2) fixes for 1 October 1987` EQUALS 551

§ `The published figure by date [pub]`

#ASSERT REFUSED `the full monthly minimum wage of an employee aged 18 or over on` (YMD 2025 3 31) BECAUSE "no source held by this model prints the full monthly minimum wage before 1 April 2025"
#ASSERT `the full monthly minimum wage of an employee aged 18 or over on` (YMD 2025 4 1) EQUALS 6_247.67
#ASSERT `the full monthly minimum wage of an employee aged 18 or over on` (YMD 2026 1 1) EQUALS 6_247.67
#ASSERT `the full monthly minimum wage of an employee aged 18 or over on` (YMD 2026 3 31) EQUALS 6_247.67
#ASSERT `the full monthly minimum wage of an employee aged 18 or over on` (YMD 2026 4 1) EQUALS 6_443.85
#ASSERT `the full monthly minimum wage of an employee aged 18 or over on` (YMD 2026 10 1) EQUALS 6_443.85
#ASSERT `the full monthly minimum wage of an employee aged 18 or over on` (YMD 2027 3 31) EQUALS 6_443.85
#ASSERT REFUSED `the full monthly minimum wage of an employee aged 18 or over on` (YMD 2027 4 1) BECAUSE "the full monthly minimum wage from 1 April 2027 had not been published when this model's sources were fetched"

§ `Section 2(a): who is entitled to which figure [text]`

-- Aged 18 exactly: "מלאו לו 18 שנים" is 18 completed years (F9), so the adult figure; aged 17: s 16 regulations, not held.
#ASSERT `the minimum wage under section 2(a) for` (`a monthly-paid employee aged` 18) `from` 6_443.85 EQUALS 6_443.85
#ASSERT REFUSED `the minimum wage under section 2(a) for` (`a monthly-paid employee aged` 17) `from` 6_443.85 BECAUSE "the rate for an employee under 18 is fixed by regulations under section 16, which this model does not hold; the Law itself prints none"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the minimum wage under section 2(a) for` `a monthly-paid adult in a section 17 class` `from` 6_443.85 BECAUSE "the rate for an employee in a class that section 17 regulations may cover is fixed by regulations this model does not hold"

-- "as the case may be": monthly, daily (six- or five-day week) or hourly, from a monthly figure of 18,600 ... the arithmetic:
-- monthly 18,600; six-day daily 18,600 / 25 = 744; five-day daily 18,600 x 3 / 65 = 55,800 / 65 = 858.4615..., so use 6,500 instead: 260 and 300; hourly 9,300 / 186 = 50.
#ASSERT `the minimum wage under section 2(a) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_500 EQUALS 6_500
#ASSERT `the minimum wage under section 2(a) for` `a daily-paid adult on a six-day week` `from` 6_500 EQUALS 260
#ASSERT `the minimum wage under section 2(a) for` `a daily-paid adult on a five-day week` `from` 6_500 EQUALS 300
#ASSERT `the minimum wage under section 2(a) for` `an hourly-paid adult` `from` 9_300 EQUALS 50

-- The capstone's first entry point, on a date [pub]: a monthly-paid adult, 1 April 2026.
#ASSERT `the full-time minimum wage in the unit of pay for` `a monthly-paid adult` `on` (YMD 2026 4 1) EQUALS 6_443.85
#ASSERT `the full-time minimum wage in the unit of pay for` `a monthly-paid adult` `on` (YMD 2026 3 31) EQUALS 6_247.67
#ASSERT `the full-time minimum wage in the unit of pay for` `a daily-paid adult on a six-day week` `on` (YMD 2026 4 1) EQUALS 257.754

§ `Section 2(b): a part-time employee [text]`

-- A fraction of 1/2 of 6,443.85 is 3,221.925; of 1/3 it is 2,147.95 (3 x 2,147.95 = 6,443.85).
#ASSERT `the partial minimum wage under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `at the fraction` 0.5 EQUALS 3_221.925
#ASSERT REFUSED `the partial minimum wage under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `at the fraction` 0 BECAUSE "a part-time position is a fraction greater than 0 and at most 1"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the partial minimum wage under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `at the fraction` 1.5 BECAUSE "a part-time position is a fraction greater than 0 and at most 1"
#ASSERT `the partial minimum wage under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `at the fraction` 1 EQUALS 6_443.85
#ASSERT REFUSED `the partial minimum wage under section 2(b) for` `a daily-paid adult on a six-day week` `from` 6_443.85 `at the fraction` 0.5 BECAUSE "the Law does not say what a fraction of a position does to a daily or an hourly minimum wage; only the monthly basis is answered"

-- Fork F1, hours. Hand-worked, month of 6,443.85:
--   93 hours against a full position of 186: A = 93/186 = 1/2 -> 3,221.925; B = 93/186 -> 3,221.925. They agree.
--   62 hours against 186: 1/3 -> 2,147.95 on both readings.
--   93 hours against 200: A = 93/200 = 0.465 -> 6,443.85 x 0.465 = 2,577.54 + 386.631 + 32.21925 = 2,996.39025;
--                         B = 93/186 = 1/2 -> 3,221.925. They differ.
--   200 hours against 186: more than a full position -> full figure 6,443.85 on both readings.
#ASSERT `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (93 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 186)) `read` `by the hours of a full position customary at the workplace` EQUALS 3_221.925
#ASSERT `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (93 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 186)) `read` `by the 186 hours of a month that section 1 implies` EQUALS 3_221.925
#ASSERT `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (93 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 186)) `read` `decline where the readings differ` EQUALS 3_221.925
#ASSERT `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (62 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 186)) `read` `decline where the readings differ` EQUALS 2_147.95
#ASSERT `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (93 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 200)) `read` `by the hours of a full position customary at the workplace` EQUALS 2_996.39025
#ASSERT `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (93 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 200)) `read` `by the 186 hours of a month that section 1 implies` EQUALS 3_221.925
#ASSERT REFUSED `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (93 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 200)) `read` `decline where the readings differ` BECAUSE "the Law does not decide how to measure the fraction of a part-time position by hours: reading A uses the hours of a full position customary at the workplace, reading B the 186 hours of s 1, and they give different answers here"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (93 `worked against a full position of` NOTHING) `read` `decline where the readings differ` BECAUSE "the Law does not decide how to measure the fraction of a part-time position by hours: reading A uses the hours of a full position customary at the workplace, reading B the 186 hours of s 1, and they give different answers here"
#ASSERT REFUSED `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (93 `worked against a full position of` NOTHING) `read` `by the hours of a full position customary at the workplace` BECAUSE "the fraction of the position needs the hours of a full position customary at the workplace (section 2(a)), and none were given"
#ASSERT `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (93 `worked against a full position of` NOTHING) `read` `by the 186 hours of a month that section 1 implies` EQUALS 3_221.925
#ASSERT `the minimum wage of a part-time position under section 2(b) for` `a monthly-paid adult` `from` 6_443.85 `on` (200 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 186)) `read` `decline where the readings differ` EQUALS 6_443.85

-- The capstone's second entry point, on a date [pub]: 93 of 186 hours on 1 April 2026 is 6,443.85 / 2.
#ASSERT `the monthly minimum wage of a part-time employee` `a monthly-paid adult` `on` (YMD 2026 4 1) `working` (93 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 186)) `read` `decline where the readings differ` EQUALS 3_221.925
-- On 31 March 2026 the figure was 6,247.67; 6,247.67 / 2 = 3,123.835.
#ASSERT `the monthly minimum wage of a part-time employee` `a monthly-paid adult` `on` (YMD 2026 3 31) `working` (93 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 186)) `read` `decline where the readings differ` EQUALS 3_123.835
#ASSERT REFUSED `the monthly minimum wage of a part-time employee` `a monthly-paid adult` `on` (YMD 2027 4 1) `working` (93 `worked against a full position of` (JUST 186)) `read` `decline where the readings differ` BECAUSE "the full monthly minimum wage from 1 April 2027 had not been published when this model's sources were fetched"

§ `Section 2(c): absence [text]`

-- Minimum for the whole month 6,000; a quarter of the working time absent and nothing owed by any
-- arrangement: 6,000 x (1 - 0.25) = 4,500. With an arrangement paying 900 for the absent time:
-- 4,500 + 900 = 5,400. No absence: 6,000. All of it absent, the arrangement paying 1,200: 0 + 1,200.
#ASSERT `the minimum wage after an absence, from` 6_000 `with absence of` 0.25 `and arrangement payment` NOTHING EQUALS 4_500
#ASSERT `the minimum wage after an absence, from` 6_000 `with absence of` 0.25 `and arrangement payment` (JUST 900) EQUALS 5_400
#ASSERT `the minimum wage after an absence, from` 6_000 `with absence of` 0 `and arrangement payment` NOTHING EQUALS 6_000
#ASSERT `the minimum wage after an absence, from` 6_000 `with absence of` 1 `and arrangement payment` (JUST 1_200) EQUALS 1_200
#ASSERT REFUSED `the minimum wage after an absence, from` 6_000 `with absence of` 1.5 `and arrangement payment` NOTHING BECAUSE "the fraction of the working time absent is between 0 and 1"

§ `Section 3: the pay that counts [text]`

GIVETH A `Pay for a regular working day`
`a pay with a basic wage, a cost-of-living supplement, a fixed supplement and the excluded kinds` MEANS
    `Pay for a regular working day` OF
        (LIST
            (`A pay component` OF `a basic wage or a combined wage`, 5_000),
            (`A pay component` OF `a cost-of-living supplement not included in a combined wage`, 300),
            (`A pay component` OF `a fixed supplement paid by reason of the work`, 800),
            (`A pay component` OF `a family supplement`, 300),
            (`A pay component` OF `a seniority supplement`, 200),
            (`A pay component` OF `a supplement for shift work`, 150),
            (`A pay component` OF `a measured, agreed, fixed or group premium`, 250),
            (`A pay component` OF `a thirteenth salary`, 400),
            (`A pay component` OF `an annual grant`, 100),
            (`A pay component` OF `a refund of expenses, including board, per diem and travel`, 350)),
        (JUST 5_000)

GIVETH A `Pay for a regular working day`
`a pay with a basic wage and a fixed supplement and no cost-of-living supplement` MEANS
    `Pay for a regular working day` OF
        (LIST
            (`A pay component` OF `a basic wage or a combined wage`, 5_000),
            (`A pay component` OF `a fixed supplement paid by reason of the work`, 800)),
        (JUST 5_000)

GIVETH A `Pay for a regular working day`
`a pay with a basic wage, a fixed supplement and an unnamed component` MEANS
    `Pay for a regular working day` OF
        (LIST
            (`A pay component` OF `a basic wage or a combined wage`, 5_000),
            (`A pay component` OF `a cost-of-living supplement not included in a combined wage`, 300),
            (`A pay component` OF `a component the text does not name`, 700)),
        (JUST 5_000)

GIVETH A `Pay for a regular working day`
`a pay with only an expense refund` MEANS
    `Pay for a regular working day` OF
        (LIST
            (`A pay component` OF `a refund of expenses, including board, per diem and travel`, 350)),
        (JUST 4_000)

GIVETH A `Pay for a regular working day`
`a pay with only a fixed supplement and no regular pay given` MEANS
    `Pay for a regular working day` OF
        (LIST
            (`A pay component` OF `a fixed supplement paid by reason of the work`, 800)),
        (NOTHING)

-- Basic 5,000 + cost-of-living 300 + fixed supplement 800 count: 6,100. Family 300, seniority 200,
-- shift 150, premium 250, 13th salary 400, annual grant 100 and expenses 350 do not. Both basic and
-- cost-of-living are present, so neither reading of s 3(d) applies it, and the readings agree.
#ASSERT `the pay that counts for` `a pay with a basic wage, a cost-of-living supplement, a fixed supplement and the excluded kinds` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` EQUALS 6_100
#ASSERT `the pay meets the minimum wage:` `a pay with a basic wage, a cost-of-living supplement, a fixed supplement and the excluded kinds` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` `against` 6_100 EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `the pay meets the minimum wage:` `a pay with a basic wage, a cost-of-living supplement, a fixed supplement and the excluded kinds` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` `against` 6_100.01 EQUALS FALSE

-- Basic 5,000 + fixed supplement 800, no cost-of-living supplement. Reading P (neither present): s 3(d)
-- does not apply: 5,800. Reading L (either missing): s 3(d) applies: the regular pay without additions, 5,000.
#ASSERT `the pay that counts for` `a pay with a basic wage and a fixed supplement and no cost-of-living supplement` `read` `only when the pay has neither a basic or combined wage nor a cost-of-living supplement` `and` `decline where the readings differ` EQUALS 5_800
#ASSERT `the pay that counts for` `a pay with a basic wage and a fixed supplement and no cost-of-living supplement` `read` `when the pay lacks either of them` `and` `decline where the readings differ` EQUALS 5_000
#ASSERT REFUSED `the pay that counts for` `a pay with a basic wage and a fixed supplement and no cost-of-living supplement` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` BECAUSE "sections 3(b) and 3(d) do not decide which pay counts here: the allowed readings (whether section 3(d) applies, whether an unlisted component counts) give different amounts"
-- Against a minimum of 4,000 both readings land above it: the question is answered. Against 5,500 they split: declined.
#ASSERT `the pay meets the minimum wage:` `a pay with a basic wage and a fixed supplement and no cost-of-living supplement` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` `against` 4_000 EQUALS TRUE
#ASSERT `the pay meets the minimum wage:` `a pay with a basic wage and a fixed supplement and no cost-of-living supplement` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` `against` 5_800.01 EQUALS FALSE
#ASSERT REFUSED `the pay meets the minimum wage:` `a pay with a basic wage and a fixed supplement and no cost-of-living supplement` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` `against` 5_500 BECAUSE "sections 3(b) and 3(d) do not decide whether this pay meets the minimum: the allowed readings put it on different sides of the minimum"

-- An unnamed component of 700 (F3): counted, 5,000 + 300 + 700 = 6,000; not counted, 5,300.
#ASSERT `the pay that counts for` `a pay with a basic wage, a fixed supplement and an unnamed component` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `it counts` EQUALS 6_000
#ASSERT `the pay that counts for` `a pay with a basic wage, a fixed supplement and an unnamed component` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `it does not count` EQUALS 5_300
#ASSERT REFUSED `the pay that counts for` `a pay with a basic wage, a fixed supplement and an unnamed component` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` BECAUSE "sections 3(b) and 3(d) do not decide which pay counts here: the allowed readings (whether section 3(d) applies, whether an unlisted component counts) give different amounts"

-- Only an expense refund: nothing counts under s 3(b), but neither basic nor cost-of-living is present, so
-- both readings apply s 3(d): the regular pay without additions, 4,000.
#ASSERT `the pay that counts for` `a pay with only an expense refund` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` EQUALS 4_000
-- Only a fixed supplement and no regular pay given: reading P and L both apply s 3(d), which needs a regular pay.
#ASSERT REFUSED `the pay that counts for` `a pay with only a fixed supplement and no regular pay given` `read` `decline where the readings differ` `and` `decline where the readings differ` BECAUSE "section 3(d) calculates the pay by the regular wage without additions, and none was given"
